On macOS, Ladybird now keeps scrollbars shown whenever NSScroller’s
preferredScrollerStyle is legacy. That’s how all three engines choose
between overlay and classic scrollbars there: Blink in the function
OsSettingsProviderMac::PrefersOverlayScrollbars(), Gecko in its lookup
of UseOverlayScrollbars in widget/cocoa/nsLookAndFeel.mm, and WebKit in
ScrollerStyle::recommendedScrollerStyle(). Run on this Mac (a MacBook
with only its trackpad), Chrome 158 gave a page a 15px classic scrollbar
with “Show scroll bars” set to “Always”, and overlay scrollbars with
“Automatically” or “When scrolling”. Playwright’s WebKit 26.5 build
showed overlay scrollbars in all three modes, so Safari’s behavior
wasn’t verified by running it. On Linux and Windows, Ladybird keeps them
shown by default, as Chrome does with its classic scrollbars there;
Firefox on GTK follows the desktop’s overlay-scrollbar setting instead
(the UseOverlayScrollbars lookup in widget/gtk/nsLookAndFeel.cpp).
